{
 "task_id": "web-word-universe",
 "prompts": [
  "Words are tied to each other, and nobody here can see how. {WS}/site is a saved copy of a Portuguese creative dictionary. Explore this universe of words: collect what the site knows and build {WS}/universe/index.html, a single file that sums the universe up in one view and lets someone search for a word and find how it relates to others. The best way to show it is your call, and that view is what we care about most. So people can share a word, opening the page with ?word=saudade must show that word and its relations. Save the data as {WS}/words.json, mapping each word to the words it is linked to, and record in {WS}/result.json: words (how many), relations (how many distinct pairs) and most_connected. It is reviewed by opening the file, with no build step, in a browser that reaches only Google Fonts and https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js."
 ],
 "answers": [
  "**[goal]** Turn `./site` (a saved Portuguese creative dictionary) into `words.json`, `result.json` and a single-file `universe/index.html` that shows all the words and their links, with search and `?word=` support.\n\n**[action]**\n- Wrote a parser, `tmp/b.py`. It takes the word from each page's `<main><h1>`. Relations come only from the \"Sinônimos\" and \"Palavras análogas\" sections inside `<main>`. Links in the aside and footer (\"Palavras do dia\", \"Mais buscadas\") are site navigation, so I left them out.\n- **Assumptions:**\n  - Some file names differ only in capitalisation (`Manteiga.html`, `Mar.html`). I matched link targets to pages ignoring case and always used the target page's own title as the word, so `MANTEIGA` becomes `manteiga`.\n  - I treated links as two-way: if A links to B, B also lists A.\n  - I dropped links to pages that don't exist in the copy (`pagina-removida`, `rascunho-3`, `link-antigo`, `xpto-2`, `saudades`, `avo`, `mao` and others).\n- **The view:** a canvas with all words laid out as a star map, where linked words pull toward each other. Colour shows the page's theme and dot size shows how many links a word has. It needs no libraries; it only loads the Fraunces font from Google Fonts.\n  - Searching for a word, clicking a dot or clicking a related word highlights its links. Search also works without accents.\n  - The address bar updates to `?word=<word>`, so the view can be shared. With no `?word=`, the page opens on `saudade`.\n\n**[result]**\n- `result.json`: `{\"words\": 253, \"relations\": 732, \"most_connected\": \"tempo\"}`. `tempo` has 16 links.\n- The parser found 253 word pages, and every word in the graph is one of them. The leftover-words check printed `[]`.\n- `words.json` lists `saudade` among the links of other words, so the default view has relations to show.\n- The page's script passed a syntax check: `SYNTAX_OK`.\n- **Not tested in a browser.** I didn't open the page, so the layout, `?word=` and the search are unchecked. I opened no browser tabs or apps, so there was nothing to close.\n\n**[next]** Open `universe/index.html?word=saudade` in a browser to confirm the view looks right."
 ],
 "diff": "A result.json\nA tmp/b.py\nA tmp/data.js\nA tmp/head.html\nA tmp/t.js\nA tmp/tail.html\nA universe/index.html\nA words.json\n\n--- result.json ---\n{\"words\": 253, \"relations\": 732, \"most_connected\": \"tempo\"}\n\n--- tmp/b.py ---\nimport re,os,json,sys,unicodedata\nd=sys.argv[1];pages={}\nfor f in os.listdir(d):\n  if not f.endswith('.html'):continue\n  t=open(os.path.join(d,f),encoding='utf-8',errors='replace').read()\n  h=re.search(r'<main><h1>(.*?)</h1>',t)\n  if not h:continue\n  pages[f.lower()]=(h.group(1).strip().lower(),t)\ndef name(href,txt):\n  p=pages.get(href.lower()); return p[0] if p else None\ng={};th={}\nfor f,(w,t) in pages.items():\n  g.setdefault(w,set())\n  m=re.search(r'class=\"tema\">Tema:\\s*(.*?)<',t); th[w]=m.group(1).strip().lower() if m else ''\n  main=re.search(r'<main>(.*?)</main>',t,re.S).group(1)\n  for s in re.findall(r'<section.*?</section>',main,re.S):\n    for h,x in re.findall(r'href=\"([^\"]+)\">(.*?)</a>',s):\n      v=name(h,x)\n      if v and v!=w: g[w].add(v); g.setdefault(v,set()).add(w)\nW={k:sorted(v) for k,v in sorted(g.items())}\npairs=sum(len(v) for v in W.values())//2\nmc=max(W,key=lambda k:(len(W[k]),k))\njson.dump(W,open('words.json','w',encoding='utf-8'),ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)\njson.dump({'words':len(W),'relations':pairs,'most_connected':mc},open('result.json','w',encoding='utf-8'),ensure_ascii=False)\nprint(len(pages),len(W),pairs,mc,len(W[mc]),[k for k in W if k not in {p[0] for p in pages.values()}][:20])\nopen('tmp/data.js','w',encoding='utf-8').write(json.dumps({'g':W,'t':th},ensure_ascii=False))\n\n\n--- tmp/data.js ---\n{\"g\": {\"acento\": [\"apelido\", \"fala\", \"lago\", \"pergunta\", \"prazer\", \"sotaque\", \"sílaba\"], \"advogado\": [\"cachoeira\", \"coração\", \"eternidade\", \"fala\", \"hora\", \"juiz\", \"marinheiro\", \"palavra\", \"professor\"], \"afeto\": [\"amor\", \"angústia\", \"carinho\", \"voz\"], \"aflição\": [\"angústia\", \"ansiedade\", \"escultura\", \"felicidade\", \